  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through Friday)\nIssued at 242 PM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nA southwesterly flow pattern aloft will continue over southeast New \nMexico and much of west Texas on Monday between upper-level ridging \nover the Gulf Coast states/Mississippi Valley and an upper-level \ntrough that will continue to dig across the western CONUS. A surface \ntrough meanwhile extends from the central Plains and into southeast \nNew Mexico and far west Texas. Weak shortwave impulses embedded \nwithin the southwesterly flow aloft and convergence along the \nsurface trough will aid in the development of isolated showers and \nthunderstorms over portions of the Upper Trans Pecos, Davis \nMountains, Marfa/Stockton Plateaus, and Big Bend/Presidio Valley \nMonday afternoon and evening. An upper-level low deepening over \nNevada Monday night will slowly lift across Utah Tuesday into \nTuesday night. Deep layer moisture will increase over portions of \nsoutheast New Mexico and much of west and southwest Texas underneath \nthe persistent southwesterly flow aloft ahead of this feature. A \nsurface trough also remains oriented from far southeast New Mexico \ninto Davis Mountains/Big Bend. Shortwave impulses embedded within \nthe southwesterly flow aloft should initiate scattered to numerous \nshowers and thunderstorms along and to the east of the surface \ntrough axis Tuesday afternoon and evening. We will maintain a decent \nchance (30-60%) of showers/storms over far southeast New Mexico, \nmuch of the Permian Basin, Upper Trans Pecos, Davis Mountains/Marfa \nPlateau, and Big Bend region. Modest CAPE values between 1000-1500 \nJ/KG and bulk shear values up to 25-35 kt could support a few \norganized storms capable of producing damaging gusts and/or hail \nTuesday afternoon/evening. High PWAT values up to 1.4-1.7 inches \nwill also support heavy rainfall and localized flash flooding with \nthe stronger storms that develop. Temperatures remain around 10 \ndegrees or so above normal both Monday and Tuesday with highs mostly \nin the upper 80s to mid 90s with morning lows in the mid 60s to \nlower 70s. \n\nAn active weather pattern looks to persist Wednesday and potentially \nthrough the end of the week. Ensemble guidance generally shows the \nupper-level low slowly moving eastward toward Colorado Wednesday \nbefore eventually phasing with another trough in the northern stream \nflow over the Plains by Thursday. A moist southwesterly flow aloft \nremains prevalent over much of our forecast area both days and \npersistent shortwave disturbances lifting over the very moist \nairmass over our region will support a continued good (30-60%) \nchance of showers and thunderstorms Wednesday and Thursday, with the \nbest chances focused over the Permian Basin, Trans Pecos, Davis \nMountains, and Big Bend region. The pattern looks supportive of \nheavy rainfall and localized flash flooding where the deepest \nmoisture axis sets up both days. A cold front still looks on track \nto push through much of the forecast area late Thursday into early \nFriday morning. There will be a continued chance of showers and \nthunderstorms as the cold front moves into our region on Friday. \nTemperatures will trend much cooler behind the front with lingering \nrain chances/cloud cover with highs currently forecast to range in \nthe upper 60s to mid 70s. Overnight lows may fall into the 40s and \n50s by Friday night.",
